body:
How one [[Class]] can be connected to another. Three relationships, and picking the wrong one
is the most common design mistake in the course.

| Relationship | Reads as | In C++ |
|---|---|---|
| **Inheritance** | *is-a* | `class Dog : public Animal` |
| **Composition / aggregation** | *has-a* | a data member of the other class type |
| **Association** | *uses-a* | takes it as a [[Parameter]], or holds a [[Pointer]] to it |

## is-a: inheritance

A **super class** (base, parent) is the general one; a **sub class** (derived, child) is the
specific one. The subclass inherits every member of the superclass and then adds or replaces.
[[Classes]] has the ladder: super class → sub class → [[Object]].

```c++
class Animal {
public:
	void breathe();
	virtual void speak();
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
	void speak();      // overriding: same signature, new body
	void fetch();      // new, only dogs have it
};
```

A `Dog` *is an* `Animal`, so anywhere an `Animal` is expected a `Dog` works. Replacing an
inherited method is [[Overriding]]; giving one class two functions with the same name and
different parameters is [[Overloading]], which is unrelated and happens without any
inheritance at all. Do not mix them up: overloading is resolved at compile time by the
argument types, overriding at run time by what the object actually is.

Private members are inherited but not accessible; that is what the interface/implementation
line means from the subclass's side. See [[Interface]] and [[Encapsulation]].

## has-a: composition

The other one, and usually the right one. A `Car` has an `Engine`; it is not a kind of
engine. In C++ that is just a data member:

```c++
class Car {
private:
	Engine motor;
};
```

The test is the sentence. If "a Car is an Engine" sounds wrong, do not inherit. Beginners
reach for inheritance because it is the new toy, and produce hierarchies that have to be
unpicked later. [[INTRODUCTION TO SOFTWARE ENGINEERING]] makes the argument properly: the
[[Object Oriented Approach]] cares about [[Coupling and Cohesion]], and inheritance is the
tightest coupling there is, because a subclass depends on its parent's internals.

Class relationships are what [[OOM|UML class diagrams]] are drawn to show, which is where this
material goes next.

